  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 253 AM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nTuesday, Southwesterly flow will still be prominent over the\narea as the trough starts to move eastward. This will allow\ndrier air aloft to enter over the area. A weak surface low\npressure system will likely be going through the area within the\nearly morning hours. This should allow cooler temperatures into\nthe 70s and a slight increase in cloud cover north of I-70.\nHowever, a secondary surface low pressure system will likely\nform to the west during the evening hours. This should lead into\nour first chances for evening showers to occur throughout the\nperiod as this could bring low level moisture into the area from\nthe south/southwest. There is medium confidence (40-60%) that\nthis will happen.\n\nWednesday, it is very likely that lingering showers from Tuesday\nwill occur throughout the day as the upper-level trough starts\nto move into the Four Corners region. Though later in the day,\nthere is a possibility that an afternoon thunderstorm could\nhappen as a cold front could pass through. The limiting factor\nfor storms will be elevated surface based CAPE over 600 J/kg.\nThere is low confidence (25%) that this will occur. If storms do\noccur it will be weak. Otherwise, it will be cloudy and\nscattered showers over the area.\n\nThursday, the consistency of the upper-level trough starts to\nbecome unclear as the axis moves over the area. Guidance shows\nsome uncertainty with the timing and could either speed up or\nslow down as another cold front moves through. If the trough\nspeeds up, we could expect the cold front to be in the early\nmorning hours before dawn with lower afternoon temperatures. If\nthe cold front slows down, we can then expect to see slightly\nwarmer temperatures with the front occurring in the afternoon. \nIf precipitation occurs, it will likely be west of Hwy 27.",
